export const essay = {
  "en": {
    "title": "Why Syrtis",
    "dek": "Renaming TokenBar: derivation, candidates, and planetary time. Decided 17 July 2026.",
    "html": "<h2>An out-of-place name</h2><p>My other open-source projects include coralline (a red alga) and pilotfish (the pilot fish). They are lowercase, single words, marine organisms, and metaphors for what the software does, without explaining themselves.</p><p>Placed alongside them, TokenBar was clearly from a different tradition. It spelled out its mechanics: token plus bar. You saw it, understood it, and that was the end of it. There is nothing wrong with a functional name, but it leaves no resonance.</p><p>To find a replacement, I had to clarify what this tool actually does. It comes down to a single idea:</p><blockquote>Tiny things accumulating, grain by grain, settling over time into a visible shape.</blockquote><p>Every token consumed in a prompt is too small to notice on its own. Day by day, however, those numbers add up: the menu-bar counter grows, and the 3D contribution graph rises cell by cell. The cat in the menu bar—an element brought over from tokcat—spins faster as usage climbs. The app reads the session logs AI coding tools already write on your computer, tracking tokens, costs, and subscription quotas across more than 25 tools. There is no account, no telemetry, and no uploaded usage data.</p><p>The name needed to be about that process of accumulation.</p><h2>Etymology: a verb for dragging</h2><p>Syrtis comes from the ancient Greek Σύρτις, derived from the verb σύρω (<em>syrō</em>), meaning to drag, pull, or sweep along.</p><p>The root is exact. A shoal does not appear from nowhere; currents drag sand along the seabed until the water loses its momentum, dropping the grains in place. Day after day, sediment gathers until it breaches the surface.</p><p>The Greeks used \"the dragged place\" to describe such shallow formations, specifically the two notorious gulfs off North Africa: Syrtis Major and Syrtis Minor, known today as the Gulf of Sidra in Libya and the Gulf of Gabès in Tunisia. Ancient accounts describe their shallows as treacherous waters where ships ran aground at the rise and fall of the tide and were rarely saved.</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/ptolemy-1420.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Africa in a Greek manuscript of Ptolemy's <em>Geography</em>, about 1420. ΣΥΡΤ ΜΙΚΡ and ΣΥΡΤ ΜΕΓΑΛΗ mark the Lesser and the Greater Syrtis. British Library, Burney MS 111, f. 52r. Public domain.</figcaption></figure><h2>The first layer: the fear of sailors</h2><p>Ancient mariners feared the Syrtis.</p><p>Its danger lay not in a violent appearance, but in its calm deception. The surface looked tranquil and deep enough, yet sandbanks shifted unpredictably underneath. A passage that was clear one season could ground a vessel the next. With wide tides, ships ran aground at the ebb and could not break free.</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/gabes-2024.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Gulf of Gabès, the ancient Syrtis Minor, photographed from the International Space Station on 2 August 2024. NASA (iss071e414023), public domain.</figcaption></figure><p>In Acts 27:17, during Paul's voyage across the Mediterranean, the crew ran into a violent storm. Fearing they would run aground on the Syrtis, they lowered the gear and let the ship drive before the wind. It is one of the best-known ancient mentions of that navigational fear.</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/luyken-1708.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Jan Luyken, <em>The Shipwreck of Paul on the Island of Melite</em> (Acts 27:41), etching, 1708. Rijksmuseum, CC0.</figcaption></figure><p>The Syrtis was terrain you had to monitor continuously, or pay the price. For a tool tracking usage, that metaphor is already complete: usage is an invisible cost that accumulates beneath quiet waters until you run aground. Yet the name carries a second layer.</p><h2>The second layer: Huygens's hourglass</h2><p>On 28 November 1659, the Dutch astronomer Christiaan Huygens turned his telescope toward Mars and sketched a large dark marking on its surface. It was the first documented surface feature of another planet, later known as the Hourglass Sea.</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/huygens-1659.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Huygens's drawing of Mars, 28 November 1659, south at the top, as reproduced in Percival Lowell's <em>Mars</em> (1894). Public domain.</figcaption></figure><p>Huygens then tracked the marking across several nights. On 1 December 1659, noting that the dark patch returned to roughly the same position each night, he concluded that Mars rotated on its axis in about 24 hours, much like Earth. Mars's true sidereal rotation is roughly 24 hours and 37 minutes; his estimate was off by just over half an hour.</p><blockquote>He used a surface feature to measure the time of a planet.</blockquote><p>In 1877, Giovanni Schiaparelli drew on classical geography to map Mars during its close approach to Earth. The marking Huygens had drawn became Syrtis Major.</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/schiaparelli-1888.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Schiaparelli's map of Mars, 1888, reprinted with a few names from his earlier charts. SYRTIS MAJOR is labelled at the left of the left hemisphere; south is at the top. Public domain.</figcaption></figure><p>A shallow terrestrial sandbank had lent its name to a Martian landmark—the first feature humanity ever used to measure planetary time.</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/hubble-1999.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Mars from the Hubble Space Telescope, 1999. The large dark marking is Syrtis Major; the bright basin south of it is Hellas. Credit: NASA, ESA and the Hubble Heritage Team (STScI/AURA).</figcaption></figure><h2>Mapping three layers to the product</h2><ul><li>Accumulation: currents drag sand grain by grain into a shoal; token consumption accumulates cell by cell into the 3D graph and the menu-bar counter.</li><li>Observation: hidden shallows beneath calm water run you aground if neglected; token usage and quotas remain silent overheads that demand steady observation.</li><li>Timekeeping: humanity's first landmark for measuring a planetary day; the app is fundamentally a counter charting time and daily usage.</li></ul><p>All three layers align without contrivance. Other candidates captured a single layer; Syrtis held all three.</p><p>It also retains quiet restraint. Someone seeing Syrtis will not understand it at once. They have to look it up, and once they do, they remember Huygens's sketch.</p><p>It shares the temperament of coralline and pilotfish: quiet, self-contained, waiting to be discovered.</p><h2>Candidates considered</h2><ul><li><strong>catshark</strong> (the catshark): An early frontrunner. It referenced the cat in the menu bar that spins faster with higher usage, echoed my own name (Nyanako), and shared a shark family metaphor with pilotfish. <strong>Too obvious.</strong> Cat plus shark was transparent on sight, branded only the surface, and bore no relation to what the tool does. The cat belongs in the menu bar, not on the nameplate.</li><li><strong>sandbar</strong> (a ridge of sand built by currents): The archetype of sand accumulating into a shoal, with a quiet link to the \"bar\" in TokenBar and the sandbar shark. <strong>Too ordinary.</strong> The concept was sound, but it lacked historical depth. Syrtis is the ancient Greek equivalent, carrying two thousand years of weight.</li><li><strong>shiome</strong> (Japanese for tide rip, the boundary where currents meet): The visible line formed where two ocean currents meet, read by fishermen to locate fishing grounds, and the root of the Japanese idiom for a turning point. It was the only candidate weaving my Japanese naming lineage into the product. <strong>Near miss.</strong> It spoke of confluence and turning points rather than gradual accumulation, missing the core dynamic of the tool.</li><li><strong>tombolo</strong> (Italian geographical term for a spit connecting an island to the mainland): A metaphor for connecting more than 25 disparate AI coding tools into a single view. <strong>A secondary feature, not the essence.</strong> It described integration rather than the primary accumulation of tokens, and the word felt slightly long.</li><li><strong>varve</strong> (Swedish geological term for an annual layer of glacial lake sediment): A geological structure where annual sediment layers can be counted like tree rings, an exact counterpart to the contribution graph. <strong>It belongs to glacial freshwater lakes rather than the sea, breaking the marine lineage of coralline and pilotfish.</strong></li><li><strong>spyhop</strong> (cetacean term for a whale rising vertically out of the water to look around): A visual image for glancing at a menu bar or tray icon to check usage. <strong>Contradictory.</strong> The app operates with zero telemetry, no accounts, and no uploaded logs; having \"spy\" in the name sounded wrong.</li><li><strong>remora</strong> (the suckerfish that rides sharks): A companion fish that attaches to sharks, forming a pair with pilotfish; attaching also mirrored a lightweight resident utility reading local logs. <strong>Already taken.</strong> I had already used the name for another of my projects.</li></ul><h2>Rejected for collisions</h2><ul><li><strong>atoll</strong>: A well-known macOS Dynamic Island tool already uses the name.</li><li><strong>krill</strong>: KrillinAI is a large project with that name.</li><li><strong>shoal</strong>: An existing project in the AI agent space already uses this name.</li><li><strong>baleen</strong>: Several NLP projects already use this name.</li><li><strong>nyanbar</strong>: An existing cat-themed Claude Code monitor app already uses this name.</li></ul><h2>In the end</h2><p>From catshark to Syrtis, the central criterion remained the same: a quiet name.</p><p>Not obscure, but not transparent at first glance. A good name is like a shoal beneath calm water: unseen at first, discovered upon closer inspection, and impossible to forget once known.</p><p>Beneath Syrtis lies two thousand years of maritime caution, and nights spent using a sandbank to measure the length of an alien day.</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/viking-1980.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Syrtis Major hemisphere of Mars, a mosaic of Viking Orbiter images, 1980. NASA/USGS, public domain.</figcaption></figure>"
  },
  "zh-tw": {
    "title": "為什麼是 Syrtis",
    "dek": "TokenBar 改名的完整推導，2026 年 7 月 17 日定案。",
    "html": "<h2>一個不成套的名字</h2><p>我的其他開源專案包括 coralline（珊瑚藻）與 pilotfish（領航魚）。小寫、一個字、海洋生物、隱喻功能，不主動解釋自己。</p><p>TokenBar 放在旁邊，一眼就能看出屬於另一種思維。它把功能直接拼出來：token 加上 bar。看到就懂，懂完就沒了。這種直白的名字沒有錯，只是少了一點餘味。</p><p>要改名，就得先釐清這個軟體到底在做什麼。說穿了只有一件事：</p><blockquote>無數微小的東西，一粒一粒累積，日子久了沉積成看得見的形狀。</blockquote><p>每一次與 AI 對話消耗的 token，少到不值得單獨去看。但一天一天疊加起來，選單列上的數字會逐漸增長，主畫面那張 3D 貢獻圖也會一格一格浮現。選單列上那隻承襲自 tokcat 的旋轉貓，也會隨著用量攀升越轉越快。這款軟體常駐在選單列（macOS）或系統匣（Windows），直接讀取本機上超過 25 款 AI 程式寫入的工作階段紀錄，呈現 token、花費與訂閱額度。沒有帳號、沒有遙測資料，也不會上傳任何本機資料。</p><p>因此我要找的名字，必須能講述這種緩慢累積成形的意象。</p><h2>字源：一個關於拖曳的字</h2><p>Syrtis 來自古希臘文 Σύρτις，字根是動詞 σύρω（syrō），意思是「拖、拉、拖曳」。</p><p>這個字根選得極其精準。沙洲並非憑空出現，而是水流一路拖曳著沙粒，直到水勢減緩、失去力道，沙粒便在此處落下。日復一日，沉積物層層堆聚，最終浮出水面。</p><p>希臘人以「被拖曳之地」稱呼這種淺灘地形，特別用來指稱北非外海兩片惡名昭彰的海灣：Syrtis Major 與 Syrtis Minor，也就是今日利比亞的錫德拉灣與突尼西亞的加貝斯灣。古文獻記載，這裡的水域極淺，船隻往往在潮水漲退之間擱淺在沙灘上，鮮少能脫身生還。</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/ptolemy-1420.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>約 1420 年托勒密《地理學》希臘文抄本中的非洲圖，ΣΥΡΤ ΜΙΚΡ 與 ΣΥΡΤ ΜΕΓΑΛΗ 分別標示小 Syrtis 與大 Syrtis。大英圖書館 Burney MS 111, f. 52r，公有領域。</figcaption></figure><h2>第一層：航海者的恐懼</h2><p>古代地中海的水手畏懼 Syrtis。</p><p>它的兇險不在於風浪洶湧，而在於隱蔽莫測。海面看似平靜，水深看似無虞，水面下的沙洲卻隨著水流不斷游移。今年安全的航道，明年可能就成了擱淺的陷阱。加上潮差極大，退潮時船隻會直接困在沙中動彈不得。</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/gabes-2024.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>加貝斯灣，也就是古代的小 Syrtis，國際太空站於 2024 年 8 月 2 日拍攝。NASA（iss071e414023），公有領域。</figcaption></figure><p>《使徒行傳》第 27 章記載了保羅航向羅馬時的海難：船隻在地中海遭遇暴風，水手害怕在 Syrtis 擱淺，便放下船具任船漂流。和合本譯作「又恐怕在賽耳底沙灘上擱了淺」，其中「賽耳底」就是 Syrtis 的音譯。這是古代文獻中關於擱淺恐懼最著名的記載之一。</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/luyken-1708.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Jan Luyken〈保羅在馬爾他島的船難〉（使徒行傳 27:41），1708 年蝕刻版畫。Rijksmuseum，CC0。</figcaption></figure><p>換句話說，Syrtis 是一片必須持續觀測、否則就會付出代價的地形。對於一款監控用量的工具而言，暗沙與擱淺的隱喻到這裡已經十分貼切：用量是平靜水面下的隱形成本，若不留心觀測，隨時可能超額受困。但這個字還有第二層意義。</p><h2>第二層：惠更斯的沙漏</h2><p>1659 年 11 月 28 日，荷蘭天文學家惠更斯（Christiaan Huygens）將望遠鏡對準火星，畫下了一幅草圖。</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/huygens-1659.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>惠更斯 1659 年 11 月 28 日的火星素描，上方為南；翻印自 Percival Lowell《Mars》（1894）。公有領域。</figcaption></figure><p>圖中有一塊沙漏狀的深色暗斑。這是人類文獻中第一筆被記錄下來的外星地表特徵，這塊地形後來被稱為「沙漏海」。</p><p>隨後，惠更斯在連續數個夜晚持續追蹤這塊暗斑。他在 1659 年 12 月 1 日記錄下觀察結果：這處暗斑每隔大約 24 小時就會回到相近的位置。他由此推算出，火星的自轉週期與地球相當，大約也是 24 小時。火星現代測得的恆星自轉週期約為 24 小時 37 分，惠更斯當年的推估誤差僅有半個多小時。</p><blockquote>他用一片地貌，量出了一顆星球的時間。</blockquote><p>兩百多年後的 1877 年，義大利天文學家斯基亞帕雷利（Giovanni Schiaparelli）在火星大衝期間繪製火星地圖，從古代地球地理中借用了名稱。惠更斯畫下的那塊暗斑，從此被正式命名為 Syrtis Major。</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/schiaparelli-1888.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>斯基亞帕雷利 1888 年的火星圖（翻印本，補上他早期圖中的部分地名）。SYRTIS MAJOR 標在左半球左側，圖的上方為南。公有領域。</figcaption></figure><p>一片地球上的流沙暗灘，名字跨越到了火星，成為人類歷史上第一個用來計量外星時間的觀測地標。</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/hubble-1999.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>哈伯太空望遠鏡 1999 年拍攝的火星。大片暗色地貌就是 Syrtis Major，其南方明亮的圓形盆地是 Hellas。來源：NASA、ESA 與 Hubble Heritage Team（STScI/AURA）。</figcaption></figure><h2>對位：三層意象如何對上產品</h2><ul><li>沉積：水流拖曳沙粒，一粒一粒堆聚成洲；每一次 token 的消耗，一格一格累積成 3D 貢獻圖與選單列上的數字。</li><li>觀測：平靜水面下的暗沙，若不持續監看便會擱淺；模型額度與花費皆是隱形成本，缺乏觀測便會超支受阻。</li><li>計時：人類第一個用來測量行星自轉的地標；這款軟體本質上就是一台記錄每日時間與用量的計數器。</li></ul><p>三層意象毫無牽強。這也是我最終選擇它的原因：其他候選名稱各自切中一層，而 Syrtis 則將三者融為一體。</p><p>此外，它還保有一種安靜的克制。初次看見 Syrtis 的人不會立刻明白含意，需要稍作查詢；而一旦查過，就會記住惠更斯的草圖與那段故事。</p><p>這正是 coralline 與 pilotfish 所共享的特質：安靜、不自我宣傳，等待使用者自行發現。</p><h2>候選過的名字</h2><ul><li><strong>catshark</strong>（貓鯊）：最早幾乎定案的名字。呼應選單列上那隻隨著用量增加而越轉越快的旋轉貓，也呼應我自己的名字（Nyanako），同時與 pilotfish 共享鯊魚家族的意象。<strong>太過直白。</strong>貓加鯊一眼望穿，只停留在圖示與個人品牌的表面，與軟體的核心行為毫無關聯。貓的趣味該留在選單列上旋轉，不必扛在產品名稱上。</li><li><strong>sandbar</strong>（沙洲）：沉積意象的原型。沙粒累積成洲，同時也是「海裡的 bar」，隱約呼應舊名 TokenBar，且自然界亦有沙洲鯊（sandbar shark）。<strong>太過尋常。</strong>概念雖然準確，卻缺乏典故的歷史厚度。Syrtis 正是沙洲的古希臘文典故，承載了兩千年的份量。</li><li><strong>shiome</strong>（潮目）：海水兩股潮流交會處所形成的肉眼可見水痕，漁人藉此尋覓漁場，亦為日文慣用語中代表轉折點的詞彙。這是所有候選中唯一融入我日文命名脈絡的提案。<strong>差了一步。</strong>它呈現的是「交會與轉折」，而非微小事物的「累積與沉積」，未能切中軟體的核心動態。</li><li><strong>tombolo</strong>（連島沙洲）：地理學名詞，指將孤立島嶼與陸地連結起來的沙洲地形。用來隱喻將 25 款以上各自獨立的 AI 開發工具整合至同一介面。<strong>切中次要功能而非本質。</strong>它強調的是「整合」這項特性，而非 token 沉積的根本機制，且音節略嫌冗長。</li><li><strong>varve</strong>（紋泥層）：地質學名詞，指冰川湖底一年沉積一層的紋泥，能如年輪般逐層計數，亦是 3D 貢獻圖的地質學對照。<strong>脫離體系。</strong>紋泥屬於淡水冰川湖泊，不屬於海洋，脫離了 coralline 與 pilotfish 的海洋生物家族脈絡。</li><li><strong>spyhop</strong>（浮窺）：鯨豚垂直探出海面環視四周的行為學術語。畫面感極佳，十分貼合偶爾探頭瞥一眼選單列監看用量的動作。<strong>意象衝突。</strong>這款軟體堅持零遙測、無帳號、完全不回傳本機紀錄，名稱帶有「spy」字樣顯得諷刺且容易引起誤解。</li><li><strong>remora</strong>（鮣魚）：吸附於鯊魚身上的共生魚類。與領航魚（pilotfish）恰好成對，一個在前領航，一個吸附隨行；「吸附」也精準呼應常駐選單列、被動讀取本機紀錄的輕量特性。<strong>撞名自己。</strong>這是我另一個專案已經使用的名稱。</li></ul><h2>撞名出局的名字</h2><ul><li><strong>atoll</strong>：知名 macOS Dynamic Island 工具已採用此名稱。</li><li><strong>krill</strong>：已有大型專案命名為 KrillinAI。</li><li><strong>shoal</strong>：AI agent 領域已有專案使用此名稱。</li><li><strong>baleen</strong>：自然語言處理（NLP）領域已有數個專案使用此名稱。</li><li><strong>nyanbar</strong>：已有一款同樣以貓為主題的 Claude Code 監控軟體使用此名稱。</li></ul><h2>最後</h2><p>從 catshark 到 Syrtis，幾經斟酌，核心準則始終只有一條：我需要一個安靜的名字。</p><p>不是晦澀難懂，而是不要一眼看穿。好的名字應當如同平靜水面下的暗沙：初看並不顯眼，探尋之後方知別有洞天，一旦知曉便難以忘懷。</p><p>在 Syrtis 的名字底下，埋藏著兩千年的航海戒懼，以及在數個夜晚裡，一個人藉由一片暗沙測量出一顆星球時間的專注。</p><figure><img src=\"__BASE__essay/viking-1980.webp\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Viking 軌道器影像拼接的火星 Syrtis Major 半球，1980 年。NASA／USGS，公有領域。</figcaption></figure>"
  }
} as const
